Procedural Content Generation and AI in Gaming
This in turn opens for what is being called Procedural Content Generation (PCG) which let game levels , textures and environments generate automatically.[11]
Several contemporary games leveraged AI-driven Procedural Content Generation (PCG) to create enormous, immersive environments and dynamic gameplay experiences:
No Man's Sky: Hello Games’ title is one of the shocking examples of the deployment of PCG. In itself, AI produces more than eighteen quintillion completely unique planets and their topographies, vegetation and wildlife, and various forms of climates. This gives players almost as many worlds each with the corresponding ecosystem as in their real-life universe. 
Spelunky: Another independent game where procedural creation is employed so that the levels are generated anew every time, the arrangement is random so it always looks different. 
Minecraft: As a company that opens infinite digital territories, Mojang considers artificial intelligence not only for enhancing specifics of how the landscape was generated but also for determining where buildings, miner shelters, chasms, and tombs should be located. 
Left 4 Dead: Valve Corporation developed the game for Microsoft system, it also involve a AI system, known as AI Director that would change the way that enemy spawn and level item location relative on the basis of the gameplay progress and skills.

Unity has released its new set of AI-based tools that improve game development in several ways:
Unity Muse: it is a generative AI tool that can be utilized through the Unity Editor to generate all of these game assets and environments from text prompts. Such accelerations in content creation and rapid prototyping are possible using such tools.
Unity Sentis: This allows the running of AI models directly on user devices, enhancing the ability for users to interact more realistically with environments and NPCs, as well as personalizing game experiences without any dependency on cloud services.
Unity Asset Store AI Solutions: Generative AI, machine learning and behavior AI to add those tools that further shorten development time and engage players.
These tools will allow you to be more creative, work faster, and make games that are a lot more appealing to your users. This is procedural games with little human interaction. These advancements are not only extending the size of the game worlds but also improving player immersion by providing novel experiences with every play through.[2]

Experience-Driven Reinforcement Learning and AI Tools
Experience-Driven Reinforcement Learning (EDRL) enables AI to learn through interaction and design new levels and challenges according to the behavior learned from the users of the game.
For example, when it comes to games like Super Mario Bros., which involve dynamic creation of novel levels by AI while the complexity is varied to keep engaging users in the process, researchers used Experience-Driven Reinforcement Learning (EDRL) to design levels that also consider how a game user would perform. AI learns the gamer behavior for instance, movement patterns and interactions.[1]
Examples of software tools that provide for this type of functionality are:
RL Brush: It is an AI-supported tool with which designers can speedily design and iterate game levels based on reinforcement learning.[18]
AngelScript: This script is utilized in games, for instance StarCraft II so that the game's AI may be enabled to dynamically optimize its strategies responding to actions pursued by a player.[12]
They are empowered for mixed-initiative co-creation, where game designers might team up with AI to design game levels, which surely lessens the time needed in the development process and increases playability at the same time

AI Research Using Games as Testing Grounds
Video games are active and exciting environment thus, in itself, serve as an ideal testing grounds for AI algorithms. Games are less predictable and actually more interactive and supply AI models with scenarios to try out adaptive strategies in. Currently, the intelligent NPC behaviours and the adaptive game content are developed by techniques such as Deep Q Learning, DDPG and NEAT (NeuroEvolution of Augmenting Topologies). [3][14][23]
For instance, the EISBot system employs reinforcement learning to train the best real time use of strategies in StarCraft. Games provide a means for using a variety of complex environments to test and refine AI algorithms.[10] 
With the OpenAI Five, playing Dota 2, great progress has been made in reinforcement learning and neural networks, showing that AI learns team work and sophisticated strategies in real time.[9]
AlphaStar: The method, developed by DeepMind, uses reinforcement learning to play against human players in StarCraft II, and chooses strategies depending upon dynamic competition.

AI for Personalized Content Generation and Storytelling
We have applied rank-based interactive evolution and other genetic algorithms to produce personalized game content for a specific player. Automated generation of coherent storylines through the use of automated quest generation alongside planning techniques based on genetic algorithms do not require manual scripting.[21]
With the huge language models there exist now, such as ChatGPT, it is possible to integrate with interactive storytelling for the first time, now creating dynamic, decision based stories that make the story more immersive and flexible to players’ decisions. 
Designed to speed up the time it takes to write dialogue for NPCs for script writers, programs like Ghostwriter are produced by Ubisoft to generate dialogue that is consistent for the game world.[2]
Other examples include:-
AI Dungeon: A text based game that uses GPT-3 and can use input from players to construct open ended stories. 
A game from 11 Bit Studios called Project C uses machine learning to alter player’s storytelling choices by changing its narrative path inside a story.

